Necrotising Fasciitis
Acute Urgent Care
Pain out of proportion, Rapidly spreading erythema, Subcutaneous crepitus/gas, Haemorrhagic bullae

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-041
Reason for Visit: Rapidly spreading, agonizing rash with swelling and blister formation following a minor scratch.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient complaining of excruciating pain in their leg that was completely disproportionate to a minor skin abrasion sustained yesterday. The area had rapidly become red, swollen, and was developing dark blisters. Urgent, immediate face-to-face assessment was critical to identify early signs of this fulminant, flesh-eating bacterial infection and trigger a massive emergency surgical and medical response, as waiting for a standard clinic appointment would result in extensive tissue necrosis and certain death.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I scratched my leg yesterday, and now it feels like acid is burning deep inside my muscle, and the redness is spreading by the hour!
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 24-hour history of an escalating, rapidly spreading erythematous and swollen area on the right lower leg following a minor garden scratch. The patient complains of excruciating, localized pain that is entirely out of proportion to the early physical skin findings. Over the past 4 hours, the patient has developed a high fever, severe malaise, and notes that the skin is beginning to form dark, fluid-filled blisters (haemorrhagic bullae). The spouse reports the patient has become increasingly confused and tachycardic.
Functional Status: Bedbound, groaning in severe pain, unable to bear weight.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of rapid septic shock and multi-organ failure.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 128 | Temp: 39.5 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient appears profoundly toxic, diaphoretic, and in agonizing pain.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam of the right lower leg reveals a rapidly spreading, poorly demarcated area of tense erythema and oedema that is remarkably warm to the touch. Several haemorrhagic bullae have formed centrally, with the underlying skin progressing to a greyish/purple discolouration indicating early necrosis. Crucially, gentle palpation elicits fine subcutaneous crepitus (crackling), indicating gas production by anaerobic bacteria deep in the fascial planes. Neurological testing reveals a paradoxical loss of sensation over the most necrotic area due to destruction of cutaneous nerves. Cardiovascular exam reveals tachycardia and hypotension, confirming severe sepsis. Expected investigations would include a CT or MRI showing extensive fascial thickening and gas within the subcutaneous tissue planes.
Environmental Observations: Patient's leg is elevated on pillows; clear signs of rapidly spreading infection marked with a pen by the spouse 2 hours ago.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Took oral paracetamol with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Necrotising Fasciitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Uncomplicated Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of pain out of proportion to the visible rash, haemorrhagic bullae, subcutaneous crepitus, and profound systemic toxicity (sepsis), which define a deep fascial infection.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of a high fever, blistering, and crepitus, alongside the extreme, burning cutaneous pain rather than a deep, dull ache.

Compartment Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary pathology due to the prominent infectious signs (fever, rapidly spreading erythema, gas formation), though compartment pressures can secondarily rise.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The diagnostic tetrad of pain out of proportion, rapidly spreading erythema, subcutaneous crepitus, and haemorrhagic bullae provides definitive clinical evidence of Necrotising Fasciitis. The patient is already entering septic shock. This represents an absolute surgical and medical emergency. An urgent home assessment was life-saving; recognizing the classic "pain out of proportion" and crepitus allowed for the immediate activation of Priority 1 emergency transport for life-saving, radical surgical debridement and broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics. Delaying intervention would have resulted in rapid limb loss and fatality.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Kept patient strictly Nil By Mouth (NBM) in preparation for emergency surgery. Administered Intravenous Ceftriaxone 2g stat (if available) to initiate early antimicrobial coverage. Immediately called 000 for Priority 1 Intensive Care Paramedic dispatch for aggressive fluid resuscitation and rapid transport to a tertiary surgical center.
Positive Behavioral Change: (N/A in this acute fulminant crisis).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics and surgical team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The spouse is instructed to monitor the patient's consciousness. If the patient collapses or stops breathing entirely, lay them flat and commence CPR immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: You have a severe, rapidly spreading infection deep in the tissues of your leg. This is a surgical emergency.
Clinical Course: The hospital team will immediately give you strong intravenous antibiotics and take you directly to the operating room to surgically remove the infected tissue and stop the spread.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to get out of bed, as your blood pressure is dangerously low. Keep your leg exactly as it is.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The bacteria causing this infection are producing toxins that are destroying the tissue deep under your skin, which is why the pain is so severe and the skin is blistering. Standard oral antibiotics will not work; you need emergency surgery to physically cut the infection out.
Actionable Steps: Let your spouse pack your identification and prepare the house for the paramedics. Focus on taking slow breaths while we wait.

